@meta
id: 21-17
title: One macro, every hit in the repo
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-across-files, macro-record
par: 107
minutes: 9
vscode: search across files, then open each result and do the two edits by hand
open: order.spec.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
The services went async and three spec files did not. Every test that
still reads `it('...', () => {` needs `async`, and the call on the line
below it needs `await`:

    it('refunds a paid order', () => {        →    it('refunds a paid order', async () => {
      service.refund('ord_101');              →      await service.refund('ord_101');

Seven tests across `order.spec.ts`, `customer.spec.ts` and
`refund.spec.ts`. Only `order.spec.ts` is open. `order.provider.ts` is
in the directory too and is not a spec; leave it alone.

Record the macro on the first test in the open file. Then use the
quickfix list from chapter 19 to find the rest, in every file, and play
the macro on each hit.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /pattern/ *.spec.ts` | fill the quickfix list from the matching files |
| `:cdo normal @a` | play `a` at every quickfix entry |
| `:wqa` | write every buffer and quit |

@hints
The macro should not step: no `j` at the end, no search at the start.
`:cdo` puts the cursor on each hit for you. Start it with `0` so the
column `:cdo` chooses does not matter.
--
Record on the first test, then grep. Grep after recording, not before,
so the line you already edited is no longer a match.
--
`/it(.*, () =>` to reach the first test. `qa0f(;iasync <Esc>j^iawait <Esc>q`.
Then `:vimgrep /it(.*, () =>/ *.spec.ts`, `:cdo normal @a`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    /it(.*, () => <Enter>   qa 0f(; iasync  <Esc> j^ iawait  <Esc> q
    :vimgrep /it(.*, () =>/ *.spec.ts<Enter>   :cdo normal @a<Enter>   :wqa<Enter>

Ninety-three keystrokes for fourteen edits in three files.

`:cdo` runs a command at every entry in the quickfix list, moving
between buffers as it goes, and `normal @a` is a command. That is the
whole idea. The macro is recorded once, in one file, on one line, and
the quickfix list carries it to every other place the pattern lives.
Nothing about the macro knows there are other files.

The order of operations matters. Record first, grep second. If you
grep first, the test you record on is entry one, and `:cdo` will
replay the macro on a line that already has its `async`. Recording
first means that line no longer matches by the time the list is
built.

The other thing to be careful about is what `:cdo` hands the macro. It
puts the cursor on the column where the match begins, which is the
`it`. A macro that starts with `0` does not care. A macro that assumes
a column will be wrong in a way that is hard to see across three files
you are not looking at, which is why the `:wqa` at the end deserves a
glance at `:ls` first when the change is bigger than this one.

@start order.spec.ts
import { OrderService } from './order.service';

describe('OrderService', () => {
  it('cancels a pending order', () => {
    service.cancel('ord_101');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', () => {
    expect(service.cancel('ord_102')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });

  it('finds an order by id', async () => {
    const order = await service.findOne('ord_103');
    expect(order.id).toBe('ord_103');
  });

  it('throws for an unknown id', () => {
    expect(service.findOne('ord_999')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
  });
});

@expected order.spec.ts
import { OrderService } from './order.service';

describe('OrderService', () => {
  it('cancels a pending order', async () => {
    await service.cancel('ord_101');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    await expect(service.cancel('ord_102')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });

  it('finds an order by id', async () => {
    const order = await service.findOne('ord_103');
    expect(order.id).toBe('ord_103');
  });

  it('throws for an unknown id', async () => {
    await expect(service.findOne('ord_999')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
  });
});

@start customer.spec.ts
import { CustomerService } from './customer.service';

describe('CustomerService', () => {
  it('creates a customer', () => {
    service.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });
    expect(repository.create).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('rejects a duplicate email', () => {
    expect(service.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' })).rejects.toThrow('CUSTOMER_EXISTS');
  });
});

@expected customer.spec.ts
import { CustomerService } from './customer.service';

describe('CustomerService', () => {
  it('creates a customer', async () => {
    await service.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });
    expect(repository.create).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('rejects a duplicate email', async () => {
    await expect(service.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' })).rejects.toThrow('CUSTOMER_EXISTS');
  });
});

@start refund.spec.ts
import { RefundService } from './refund.service';

describe('RefundService', () => {
  it('refunds inside the window', () => {
    service.refund('ord_104');
    expect(payments.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_104');
  });

  it('records the reason outside the window', () => {
    service.refund('ord_105');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED' }));
  });
});

@expected refund.spec.ts
import { RefundService } from './refund.service';

describe('RefundService', () => {
  it('refunds inside the window', async () => {
    await service.refund('ord_104');
    expect(payments.refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ord_104');
  });

  it('records the reason outside the window', async () => {
    await service.refund('ord_105');
    expect(repository.update).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ cancellationReason: 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED' }));
  });
});

@start order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }
}
